UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 17, Lesson 1: Friction and surfaces', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Friction is a force between surfaces that opposes sliding. Rougher contact often means more friction; smoother often means less.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nOrder likely friction high→low for a toy car: carpet; smooth wood; icy-style plastic sheet (if available).\n\nFEEDBACK\nCarpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical).\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nTest a block or shoe sole on three surfaces. Measure slide distance with the same gentle push (or ramp). Table results and conclude which surface had most friction.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Friction is a force between surfaces that opposes sliding. Rougher contact often means more friction; smoother often means less.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Test a block or shoe sole on three surfaces. Measure slide distance with the same gentle push (or ramp). Table results and conclude which surface had most friction. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Rubber soles grip tiles better than socks. Oil on a surface can reduce friction. Friction helps us walk without slipping and also warms rubbed hands.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects. Correction: Carpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical).\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Friction is a force between surfaces that opposes sliding. Rougher contact often means more friction; smoother often means less. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Friction is a force between surfaces that opposes sliding. Rougher contact often means more friction; smoother often means less.","Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded.","Skip the fair-test idea because the first guess looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip the fair-test idea because the first guess looked neat.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new investigation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Carpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical). Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["Carpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical).","Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Order likely friction high→low for a toy car: carpet; smooth wood; icy-style pla\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Carpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical). Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["Carpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical).","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with e\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data. Useful feedback uses the original observations and method.","options":["The new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Investigate or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a s\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named observations.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the observations that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Rubber soles grip tiles better than socks. Oil on a surface can reduce friction. Friction helps us walk without slipping and also warms rubbed hands.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely science misconception for this topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects. Correction: Carpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical).","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w17-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise friction.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Physical World","success_criteria":["I can use friction with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify surface and explain why it matters.","I can practise grip independently on a new prompt.","I can notice slide needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Friction is a force between surfaces that opposes sliding. Rougher contact often means more friction; smoother often means less. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Friction is a force between surfaces that opposes sliding. Rougher contact often means more friction; smoother often means less.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRubber soles grip tiles better than socks. Oil on a surface can reduce friction. Friction helps us walk without slipping and also warms rubbed hands.\n\nTRY FIRST\nOrder likely friction high→low for a toy car: carpet; smooth wood; icy-style plastic sheet (if available).\n\nFEEDBACK\nCarpet highest; wood middle; smooth plastic lowest (typical).","explain":"Friction is a force between surfaces that opposes sliding. Rougher contact often means more friction; smoother often means less.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking friction is only “bad” ignores how it helps us walk and hold objects. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Friction idea.","caption":"Friction also helps walking.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Friction</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">opposes sliding</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rough</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">often more grip</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":